\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 For most of her adult life, Jamie Panzarella ran most every day, often lost in her thoughts, barely aware of her body. \u8220 I felt like a horse \u8212  free and wild,\u8221  she said. \u8220 It was almost like dreaming.\u8221  Over the years, she completed five marathons and more than 20 half marathons.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Then, a few years ago, the sensation changed. Her toes \u8212  slowly rigidifying from a form of degenerative arthritis \u8212  no longer bent properly, and over time she became acutely aware of every excruciating footfall. \u8220 I never got the groove anymore,\u8221  said Ms. Panzarella, who is now 51 and lives in Austin, Texas. The dreamy dissociation she\u8217 d relied on for years was gone.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Her doctor soon spelled out the stakes for her body: \u8220 You have a finite bucket of miles,\u8221  she recalled him saying. \u8220 Do you want to run through them all now, or do you want to be able to go for a hike with your grandkids when you\u8217 re in your 70s?\u8221  It was time to stop running.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 For the roughly 50 million Americans who run regularly, it is rarely just exercise. It is therapy, meditation, community and identity, often all at once. When the body finally says no, the loss can be staggering.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Dimity McDowell, a co-founder of the running community }{\field{\*\fldinst{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  HYPERLINK }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  }{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 https://anothermotherrunner.com/}{\*\datafield 08d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b0200000003000000e0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b42000000680074007400700073003a002f002f0061006e006f0074006800650072006d006f007400680065007200720075006e006e00650072002e0063006f006d002f000000}}
{\fldrslt{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ul\cf1 Another Mother Runner}}}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 , published a book this year about her experience with that transition, \u8220 The 27th Mile.\u8221  Ms. McDowell went on what turned out to be her last run in January 2020, after years of ignoring hip, knee and hamstring pain, and even her doctor\u8217 s gentle suggestion that she \u8220 consider not running anymore.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 Quitting running is like contemplating a divorce or the death of a loved one,\u8221  Ms. McDowell writes in the book\u8217 s introduction, \u8220 something we think about a lot but don\u8217 t want to verbalize, lest doing so actually gives the thought momentum.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 A Layered Loss}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Those who have to leave running behind give up much more than fitness, said Justin Ross, a licensed clinical psychologist in Denver who specializes in sports and wellness. \u8220 Running is one of the most reliable forms of mood regulation that we have,\u8221  he said. Remove it, and he\u8217 s seen some clients experience increased irritability, difficulty handling stress, low mood and even mild depression. \u8220 It doesn\u8217 t matter how far or how fast you run,\u8221  Dr. Ross added. \u8220 It\u8217 s part of how you\u8217 ve come to understand yourself.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Many runners rely on the predictability of putting on shoes and heading out the door. Ms. Panzarella compared the feeling of watching runners now to seeing an ex with someone new: \u8220 I know you\u8217 re out there, I know you\u8217 ve moved on,\u8221  she said. \u8220 But I don\u8217 t want to have to watch!\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Ms. McDowell said that emotional pain caught her off guard. A psychiatrist told her that she was experiencing a kind of grief, and that she was allowed to mourn it.}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0
\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 When you say you\u8217 re a runner, people automatically assume you are disciplined, you are ambitious, you know how to set a goal and go for it,\u8221  she said. \u8220 It has this sheen about it.\u8221  Letting go of that identity, she discovered, felt like losing a close companion. \u8220 I really thought of running as one of my best friends. I was \u8216 with\u8217  running five times a week. And all of a sudden you have to say, \u8216 I have to step away from you.\u8217 \u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Sidelined Slowly, Then All at Once}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Some runners are forced to give up the sport not because of creaky knees or worn-out hips, but because of illness.}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard
\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Matt Fitzgerald, 55, an endurance athlete, author and coach who has written nearly 40 books about running (with another, \u8220 Dying to Run,\u8221  coming out in September) and finished 50 marathons, got }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ul\cf2 long Covid}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2  in 2020 and found himself unable to walk. \u8220 With most injuries, you can still find something else,\u8221  he said. But Mr. Fitzgerald found out through trial and error that exercise made him feel worse. He spent three years unable to run at all, came back partly a few years ago, and is now once again unable to run.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Years of uncertainty and increasing pain also brought running to an end for Maggie Boxey, now 47. Ms. Boxey had run two marathons and 15 half marathons and was dreaming of a 45-mile ultramarathon for her 45th birthday. But in February 2024, she went for her last run. She had been feeling exhausted after her runs for about four years, after a viral infection, during which she saw many doctors, but found no answers. \u8220 I was like, I don\u8217 t know what\u8217 s wrong with me,\u8221  she said.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 A week after her last run, she was diagnosed with myalgic encephalomyelitis, also known as chronic fatigue syndrome. She soon learned that every post-run crash over the preceding years had been a sign of the illness progressing. She has had more health setbacks since then, she said, and now uses a wheelchair and is mostly bedbound.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 Every time I moved to a new city, running was how I met new people,\u8221  she said. \u8220 It was the way I explored the world. It was my meditation. It was my socialization. Losing that, it was so hard.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The Road Ahead}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 For those navigating the loss of running, the goal shouldn\u8217 t be to immediately find something to replace it, said Jack Lesyk, a sports psychologist in Ohio. It\u8217 s to rebuild a sense of self. He described it as a seesaw: \u8220 As the runner identity fades, something else has to rise on the other side.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Dr. Ross finds that people who make this transition most successfully \u8220 tend to separate their deeper values from the specific activity,\u8221  he said. \u8220 For example, someone may believe that what they love about running is running itself, but when we dig deeper, we often find they value challenge, mastery, adventure, community, discipline or personal growth. Running was simply one expression of those values.\u8221  The task then becomes identifying new ways to express those values.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Dr. Ross admitted that many people don\u8217 t find a \u8220 perfect replacement\u8221  for running \u8212  passion rarely works that way. \u8220 I encourage people to approach this period less like they are searching for the next great love and more like they are exploring,\u8221  he said. \u8220 Try things. Stay open. Allow yourself to be a beginner again.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Mr. Fitzgerald found his next act by opening }{\field{\*\fldinst{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  HYPERLINK }{
\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  }{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 https://www.nytimes.com/2024/01/31/style/matt-fitzgerald-dream-run-camp.html}{\*\datafield }}
{\fldrslt{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ul\cf1 Dream Run Camp}}}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2
\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 , an adult running camp where he coaches runners. \u8220 If I couldn\u8217 t run,\u8221  he said, \u8220 I wanted to at least serve other runners.\u8221  Ms. McDowell has taken up long-distance hiking. Ms. Panzarella discovered tennis, a sport that demands she focus on the present moment. It gave her something running never had: no room to drift at all. \u8220 If you\u8217 re not paying attention, you\u8217 ll get hit in the face,\u8221  she said.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 None of them would claim it\u8217 s the same. But the community, the movement, the time that belongs only to them \u8212  those parts, at least, they\u8217 ve found again.}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0
\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Not everyone arrives at that place through struggle. Jim Shapiro, a 79-year-old teacher in Manhattan, ran across the United States in 1980 and then across Japan in 1986. He once ran a 24-hour race in England. But he recently had a total knee replacement \u8212  with a second one pending \u8212  and he can no longer run at all. He said he had occasionally felt jealousy watching others run, but now he mostly feels something closer to completion. He ran so many miles, for so many decades, that his body simply reached its limit.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 You can\u8217 t outrun old age,\u8221  he said. \u8220 We get a gift. And it\u8217 s a gift of uncertain duration. You make of it what you can.\u8221 }{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0
\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 PHOTO: Jamie Panzarella, 51, had to give up running a few years ago. She used to run nearly every day. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JORDAN VONDERHAAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page D6.}
